WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.100
Film Courage: As I said before we began rolling,
we have a lot of screenwriters who watch our

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.100
channel.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.230
I’m wondering if you can talk about how
you and your writing partner (Jason Dolan)

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.390
ended up getting signed with an agency?

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.970
Shawn Christensen, Co-Writer of The Vanishing
of Sidney Hall: So we were bashing each other’s

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:17.970
screenplays.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.020
We were roommates in New York.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.420
We were slumming it in Queens.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:30.640
We were 23-24-years-old and were critiquing
each other’s script and then we decided

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.339
why don’t we just write one together and
we wrote this movie, it was called SIDNEY

00:00:36.339 --> 00:00:39.559
HALL (not THE VANISHING OF SIDNEY HALL at
the time).

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:50.350
And we were interested in this idea of how
different we were when were 18 and now we’re

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.199
24 and we felt like we had changed a lot.

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:59.949
Not necessarily matured, not necessarily a
better person and we wanted to write something

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:02.430
that reflected that.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:08.450
So we wrote SYDNEY HALL, we wrote it in I
don’t know, a half a year or so?

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.360
And we emailed it back and forth to each other.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:22.330
And then a friend of mine (Paul Wesley) had
a manager and he gave it to a manager and

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:30.210
she gave it to at that time WME (William Morris
Endeavor).

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:36.829
And WME (an agent over there) read it and
really loved it and signed us.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.880
And that’s really how it was.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.749
So I had an actor friend essentially who had
a manager and really it’s Paul Wesley’s

00:01:45.749 --> 00:01:51.930
fault that I am in this industry because he
did that.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:59.539
He also is good with notes and to help with
giving me notes on some of my other screenplays.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:05.799
I think for an aspiring screenwriter, unsolicited
scripts is the problem, right?

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:09.860
You know, finding out who to give it to or
anything like that.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:17.670
I think people in the film industry…the
film industry is completely and wholly based

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.890
on relationships and knowing people.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:25.250
And you don’t have to know the big stars
or the big managers or anything like that,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:31.790
but I think any aspiring screenwriter if they
know someone who plays bit parts on a show

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.760
or something like that and that person has
a manager, an agent, I’d say that’s a

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.000
good route to go the way we did it.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:50.180
Which see if they like your script and maybe
give it to them and get it so, if the screenplay

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:55.700
goes to someone of importance who can pay
you to rewrite it or pay for it to be made

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:01.880
or make it into a movie and you know someone
who knows someone that is friends with them,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.820
they’ll read it if it comes from a friend,
as opposed to if it’s submitted just you

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.620
know out of the blue from nowhere.

